reconstructed_theorem:
For every pair of real numbers α and β satisfying 0 < α ≤ β, and for every real number h, there exists a real number μ, equal to either α or β and strictly positive, with the following properties. Define f: ℝ → ℝ by f(x) = μ x²/2. The function f is twice continuously Fréchet differentiable on ℝ. It is globally strongly convex with modulus α: for all real x,y and every t in [0,1], f((1−t)x+ty) ≤ (1−t)f(x)+t f(y)−(α/2)t(1−t)|y−x|². This asserts a lower bound α on the strong convexity modulus, not necessarily its maximal value. For all real x,y, f(y) ≤ f(x)+f′(x)(y−x)+(β/2)|y−x|². Also 0 is a global minimizer: f(0) ≤ f(x) for all real x. Here the gradient is the actual derivative represented as a real vector, so gradient f(x)=f′(x)=μx, the real inner product is multiplication, and the real norm is absolute value.
For this one choice of μ and f, define T_h(x)=x−h f′(x), and define T_*(x)=x−[2/(α+β)]f′(x). For every natural number N, including N=0, all three conclusions hold simultaneously:
(1) |T_h^[N](1)| = [max{|1−hα|, |1−hβ|}]^N;
(2) [(β−α)/(α+β)]^N ≤ |T_h^[N](1)|;
(3) |T_*^[N](1)| = [(β−α)/(α+β)]^N.
The notation T^[N] means N-fold function iteration, with T^[0] the identity. Every scalar zeroth power, including 0^0, is 1, so at N=0 both iterates are evaluated as 1 and each displayed power is 1. The assumptions imply β>0 and α+β>0, and allow α=β. There is no positivity or stability restriction on h: it may be zero, negative, or any positive real. The witness μ may depend on α, β, and h, but is chosen before N and works for all N at once; the optimal-step identity in (3) holds for that same witness even if it was selected for another value of h. The theorem constructs a one-dimensional endpoint-curvature quadratic with the stated certificates and iteration properties, starting at 1, whose distance to its minimizer 0 is the displayed norm. It makes no universal claim for arbitrary functions or arbitrary starting points.
